      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "How steep is the road?"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "Engineers must know how steep a road or staircase is before building it. In mathematics, steepness is measured by the slope of a line."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "definition of slope",
          "slope formula",
          "sign of the slope",
          "special lines",
          "parallel lines",
          "perpendicular lines",
          "collinearity by slope"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "The slope or gradient of a non-vertical line is m equals tan theta, where theta is the angle of inclination with the positive X axis.\nFor two points (x1,y1) and (x2,y2), the slope is the difference in y divided by the difference in x.\nIf theta is acute the slope is positive and the line rises; if theta is obtuse the slope is negative and the line falls.\nA horizontal line has slope zero, and the slope of a vertical line is undefined.\nTwo non-vertical lines are parallel if and only if their slopes are equal.\nTwo non-vertical lines are perpendicular if and only if the product of their slopes is minus one.\nThree points are collinear if the slope of AB equals the slope of BC."
        }
      },
